### Example 6 — Jackson 1.x (Codehaus) -> Jackson 2.x (FasterXML) with java.time

**INPUT (Jackson 1.x):**

```java
package com.acme.json;

import org.codehaus.jackson.annotate.JsonProperty;
import org.codehaus.jackson.map.ObjectMapper;
import org.codehaus.jackson.map.SerializationConfig;
import org.codehaus.jackson.map.annotate.JsonSerialize;
import org.codehaus.jackson.map.annotate.JsonSerialize.Inclusion;

import java.util.Date;

@JsonSerialize(include = Inclusion.NON_NULL)
public class AuditEvent {

    @JsonProperty("event_id")
    private String eventId;

    @JsonProperty("occurred_at")
    private Date occurredAt;

    @JsonProperty("actor")
    private String actor;

    // getters / setters
}

public class JsonConfig {
    public static ObjectMapper mapper() {
        ObjectMapper m = new ObjectMapper();
        m.configure(SerializationConfig.Feature.WRITE_DATES_AS_TIMESTAMPS, false);
        m.configure(SerializationConfig.Feature.INDENT_OUTPUT, true);
        m.setSerializationInclusion(Inclusion.NON_NULL);
        return m;
    }
}
```

**OUTPUT:**

### Migration plan
- Replace every `org.codehaus.jackson.*` import with the FasterXML equivalent.
- Replace `@JsonSerialize(include = Inclusion.NON_NULL)` with `@JsonInclude(JsonInclude.Include.NON_NULL)`.
- Replace `SerializationConfig.Feature` with `SerializationFeature`.
- Convert `AuditEvent` to a record (Jackson 2 supports record deserialisation natively).
- Replace `java.util.Date` with `java.time.Instant`.
- Register `JavaTimeModule` so `Instant` serialises as ISO-8601, not a numeric timestamp.

### File: src/main/java/com/acme/json/AuditEvent.java
```java
package com.acme.json;

import com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.JsonInclude;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.JsonProperty;

import java.time.Instant;

@JsonInclude(JsonInclude.Include.NON_NULL)
public record AuditEvent(
        @JsonProperty("event_id")    String eventId,
        @JsonProperty("occurred_at") Instant occurredAt,
        @JsonProperty("actor")       String actor
) {}
```

### File: src/main/java/com/acme/json/JsonConfig.java
```java
package com.acme.json;

import com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.JsonInclude;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.SerializationFeature;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.datatype.jsr310.JavaTimeModule;

public class JsonConfig {

    public static ObjectMapper mapper() {
        return new ObjectMapper()
                .registerModule(new JavaTimeModule())
                .disable(SerializationFeature.WRITE_DATES_AS_TIMESTAMPS)
                .enable(SerializationFeature.INDENT_OUTPUT)
                .setSerializationInclusion(JsonInclude.Include.NON_NULL);
    }
}
```

### Manual follow-ups
- Search the codebase for callers reading `getEventId()` / `getOccurredAt()` / `getActor()`. After the record migration, accessors become `eventId()` / `occurredAt()` / `actor()`.
- If any persisted JSON payloads contained numeric timestamps for `occurred_at`, add a custom deserialiser or a one-off migration step before flipping `WRITE_DATES_AS_TIMESTAMPS` in production.
- If this `ObjectMapper` is being injected into Spring MVC, prefer letting Spring Boot autoconfigure it (set `spring.jackson.*` in `application.yml`) instead of building it by hand.

### Maven / Gradle dependency changes
- Remove: `org.codehaus.jackson:jackson-core-asl`, `org.codehaus.jackson:jackson-mapper-asl`.
- Add: `com.fasterxml.jackson.core:jackson-databind` (transitively pulls `jackson-core` and `jackson-annotations`).
- Add: `com.fasterxml.jackson.datatype:jackson-datatype-jsr310` for `java.time` support.
- Pin all Jackson coordinates to the same 2.17.x (or newer) version, ideally inherited from Spring Boot's BOM.

---

### Common gotchas reference (consult silently — do not copy verbatim into output)

  - **SequencedCollection collisions (Java 21):** custom classes with
    `getFirst()` / `getLast()` returning anything other than the head/tail
    element will fail to compile when implementing `List` / `Deque`. Flag, do
    not "fix".
  - **`new URL("http://...")`:** constructor is deprecated for removal. Use
    `URI.create("http://...").toURL()`.
  - **`@PostConstruct` resolution:** in Spring 6, `jakarta.annotation.PostConstruct`
    is the only recognised one. If `javax.annotation.PostConstruct` slips
    through, the method silently never runs.
  - **`spring.factories` -> `AutoConfiguration.imports`:** Spring Boot 2.7+
    moved auto-config registration. If the project ships custom starters,
    flag for human review.
  - **Hibernate 6 implicit naming changes:** column / table names derived from
    field names may change. Do not auto-rename; flag.
  - **Jackson date defaults:** `ObjectMapper` without `JavaTimeModule` will
    throw `InvalidDefinitionException` for `Instant` / `LocalDateTime` in
    Jackson 2.10+. Always register the module.
  - **`@Transactional` import:** `org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional`
    is preferred over `jakarta.transaction.Transactional` because Spring's
    version supports rollback rules and propagation tuning.
  - **Mockito + Java 21:** dynamic agent loading now warns. If the file under
    migration is a test, note that `mockito-core` 5.x with the
    `mockito-byte-buddy-agent` dependency is required.
  - **Lombok:** if Lombok appears in the imports, retain it and use
    `@RequiredArgsConstructor` for the constructor-injection rewrite. If it
    does not, hand-write the constructor.

